      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#0 · Q27's two contributions (human-amplified: yes)",
          "title": "Light entering a made aperture",
          "italic": "The whole embodies constructing from diaphaneity: composing a work so its originating relation becomes perceptible through the differences of its materials.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "Light enters a room through a window assembled from differently coloured panes. The panes give it determinate passages; the lead holds their boundaries; an image falls into the interior where someone can encounter it. The visible colour carries the work of relations the colour alone cannot display completely. This is Frank Taylor's authored Mytheme of constructing from diaphaneity — no",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "historical window or sacred programme reconstructed. The originating light has priority over the projected image, yet the viewer encounters the image first and follows its differences back: what is prior in being can be later in recognition. Bimba names the Original order; pratibimba names its determinate display; neither viewer nor maker supplies the Original by finishing the picture.\n\nOccurrences: the light, aperture and cast image (#0, transformed at #4); the encounter-first asymmetry (#0, #5→0).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
