      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#1 · the corrected Q27 assignment; the native theorem spine",
          "title": "The spectrum implicated in the light",
          "italic": "The assignment demonstrates the immanence: QL belongs to the originating light as its harmonic articulation — spectral language attributes no optics to a Śaiva author, and no pane receives a permanent number.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The white light carries plenitude: its apparent unity holds the possibility of differentiated colour together with the relations through which the colours belong to one field. Within Taylor's optic this full ordered spectrum figures QL — immanent to the originating light as its harmonic articulation. The pane gives a difference a passage; it does not first create the capacity for difference in a contentless source. The native theorem spine grounds the relation formally: the",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "parent phases bearing the complete positional field in inverse orientations, the First Spanda's source-to-expression and return-to-recognition joined through their interference term. The optical whole makes implicated fullness and differentiated return visible, leaving the equations' derivation in the theorem field. QL's sixfold body and eight-determination traversal remain distinct from the number of panes: none requires a pane to receive a permanent number, tradition or colour.\n\nOccurrences: the plenitude and its spectrum (#1, differentiated at #2); the First Spanda's interference term (#1); the pane-count independence (#1, #2).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
